  "verbalization": "Lippiolidolic acid appears to interact with MIP through a series of molecular targets, with SNU-387 acting as a common intermediary. The evidence suggests that Lippiolidolic acid targets SNU-387, which in turn is a target of multiple compounds, including S-ethylcysteine zwitterion and 5-hydroxy-6,8-dimethoxy-2,3-dimethylbenzo[G]chromen-4-one. All these compounds ultimately have MIP as their target. This indicates a potential pathway where Lippiolidolic acid may influence MIP through SNU-387, possibly via a shared mechanism of action or by modulating the activity of the downstream targets. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of SNU-387 as a critical node in the interaction network, suggesting that Lippiolidolic acid may exert its effects on MIP through a complex, multi-step molecular mechanism involving the modulation of downstream targets.",
